{
  "requirement": "`trust_but_verify::outline::selector::parse_selector(&str)` returns\n`Result<Vec<Segment>, SelectorError>`. It checks syntax without reading a file.\nThe empty selector yields an empty vector. Each `Segment` exposes `kind`,\n`label`, and the optional one-based `ordinal`.\n\nFor `impl[Thing/Trait]/fn[run]#2`, the first label is `Label::Named` with the\ndecoded parts `Thing` and `Trait`. The second segment has kind `fn`, label\n`run`, and ordinal `Some(2)`. `Label::Hash` holds an anonymous item's\n`HASH_DIGITS` hexadecimal digits (12), normalized to lowercase. `SelectorError`\ncarries the offending `selector` and a diagnostic `message`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The structural check finds no gap.\n\n- **Path and signature.**\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/lib.rs` declares `pub mod outline`, and `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/contents.rs` declares `pub mod selector`. That gives the path `trust_but_verify::outline::selector`.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/selector.rs#fn[parse_selector]` is `pub fn parse_selector(selector: &str) -> Result<Vec<Segment>, SelectorError>`, which matches the required signature.\n- **No file read.** `parse_selector` works only on the string through `split_segments` and `parse_segment`. Neither function touches the filesystem.\n- **Empty selector.** `parse_selector` returns `Ok(Vec::new())` for an empty string.\n- **Segment shape.** `#struct[Segment]` has public fields `kind: String`, `label: Label` and `ordinal: Option<usize>`, so each segment exposes all three.\n- **Labels.**\n  - `#enum[Label]` has `Named(Vec<String>)` and `Hash(String)`.\n  - `parse_segment` splits the bracketed text on `/` and decodes each part with `decode_name`. So `impl[Thing/Trait]` becomes `Label::Named([\"Thing\",\"Trait\"])`.\n  - A `fn[run]#2` segment gives kind `fn`, label `run` and ordinal `Some(2)`. The ordinal is parsed from `#N` and must be greater than 0, so it is one-based.\n- **Hash labels.**\n  - `#const[HASH_DIGITS]` is `pub const HASH_DIGITS: usize = 12`.\n  - `parse_segment` requires exactly `HASH_DIGITS` hex digits after `@`.\n  - It stores them with `to_ascii_lowercase()`, which normalizes to lowercase.\n- **Errors.** `#struct[SelectorError]` has public `selector` and `message` fields. `SelectorError::new` fills the offending selector and a message. `parse_segment` re-wraps errors from `decode_name` with the full selector.\n\n`parse_selector` carries the `{impl}` marker for the requirement. The helpers it calls are reached from it, so they need no marker of their own. I found no unacknowledged gap, and no FIXME was needed.",
  "references": "b0f738dfbd7843d15153e67086b597b277d405410f3aa50f8f5b525efda7004d",
